<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lothair II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 835–<a href="/years/869">869</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Lothair II of Lotharingia · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> II was the second son of Emperor Lothair I and received the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-prum">Treaty of Prüm</a> in 855, a kingdom later named Lotharingia after him. His long attempt to divorce his wife Theutberga embroiled him with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. He died without legitimate heirs in 869, and his uncles divided his kingdom; the name survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lothair-ii.jpg" alt="Lothair II" width="236" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ekkehard von Aura · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lothar_II,_King_of_Lotharingie.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lothair II's grandfather was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a>, and his father, Lothair I, was crowned co-emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in 817. When the dying Lothair I divided his realm, Louis II, the eldest son, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the imperial title, Lothair II received the territory between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and Charles, still a child, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a>. Lothair I died on 19 September 855. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> brought the young king to the court of his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> for confirmation, and he probably ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> in exchange, though Louis did not keep it permanently.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a>'s position was insecure, and to establish himself he had to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutberga">Teutberga</a>, the daughter of Boso the Elder, which secured the support of her brother Hucbert. Lothair already had several children by his boyhood mistress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waldrada">Waldrada</a>, and as Hucbert's usefulness declined he dismissed Teutberga in 857. In 858 she was subjected to a trial by ordeal, in which her champion took an object from boiling water, and when his hand was judged to be healing normally she was found not guilty and Lothair's magnates forced him to take her back. A council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> in 862 allowed Lothair to marry Waldrada, and Teutberga appealed to Pope Nicholas I.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> also acted as a mediator between his uncles, helping to negotiate peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> by June 860. When his brother Charles of Provence died on 24 January 863, Lothair and Emperor Louis II partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a>, Lothair receiving the Lyonnais and the Viennois. Nicholas I then turned against him, excommunicated two of his archbishops and in 865 forced him to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutberga">Teutberga</a> back; he excommunicated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waldrada">Waldrada</a> in 866. Against the Northmen, Lothair chased a raiding party in 863 and in 867 won a victory over Rorik of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorestad">Dorestad</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>, with heavy casualties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nicholas died in 867, and his successor Adrian II was more flexible, allowing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutberga">Teutberga</a> to live apart from the king and lifting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waldrada">Waldrada</a>'s excommunication. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the summer of 869 to meet Adrian, dined with him in the Lateran and gave him many gifts. On the way back a fever and an epidemic struck his party, and he died at Piacenza, on 7 August 869. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> was crowned king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> on 9 September, but he had to partition Lotharingia with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-meerssen">Treaty of Meerssen</a>. Lothair's son Hugh, born to Waldrada, later claimed the kingdom, took authority in part of Lotharingia in 879 and was blinded by 885.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lothair II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lorraine takes its name from Lotharingia, the middle kingdom created for <b>Lothar II</b> at the partition of the Carolingian empire in 855, which stretched from Frisia to the Jura.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles the Bald and Louis the German divided the lands of their late nephew <b>Lothair II</b> between West and East Francia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Lotharingia was the kingdom created for <b>Lothair II</b> in 855, stretching from Frisia to the Vosges.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lotharingia">Duchy of Lotharingia</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Treaty of Prüm in 855 Lothair I left the northern part of Middle Francia, from Frisia through the Low Countries and the Rhineland to the Vosges, to his son <b>Lothair II</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lotharingia">Kingdom of Lotharingia</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles died childless in 863, and his brothers <b>Lothair II</b> and the emperor Louis II divided the kingdom between them.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-provence">Kingdom of Provence</a></li><li><blockquote>Shortly before his death in 855 Lothair retired to the abbey of Prüm and, by the Treaty of Prüm, divided the kingdom among his three sons: Louis II received Italy, <b>Lothair II</b> the north, which became the Kingdom of Lotharingia, and Charles the Kingdom of Provence.</blockquote><a href="/state/middle-francia">Middle Francia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Piacenza, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.05,9.7&amp;year=857" aria-label="Piacenza on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4781 573 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Lothair II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5231 843h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5231 843h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=45.05,9.7&amp;year=857">What was here in 857 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
